Experiment 4.1: Palindrome Linked List(Leetcode Problem 234)

Aim: Given the head of a singly linked list, return true if it is a palindrome or false otherwise.

Algorithm:

1.)Start with head of the linked list.
2.)Use two pointers, slow and fast, to find the middle node.
3.)Move slow one step and fast two steps at a time.
4.)Reverse the second half of the linked list.
5.)Set left to head and right to the head of the reversed second half.
6.)Compare the values of both halves one by one.
7.)If any values are different, return false.
8.)If all values match, return true.
9.)Stop when the second half is completely compared.

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    bool isPalindrome(ListNode* head) {
        ListNode *slow = head, *fast = head;

        // Find middle
        while (fast && fast->next) {
            slow = slow->next;
            fast = fast->next->next;
        }

        // Reverse second half
        ListNode *prev = nullptr;
        while (slow) {
            ListNode *next = slow->next;
            slow->next = prev;
            prev = slow;
            slow = next;
        }

        // Compare both halves
        ListNode *left = head, *right = prev;
        while (right) {
            if (left->val != right->val)
                return false;
            left = left->next;
            right = right->next;
        }

        return true;
    }
};

Experiment 4.2: Odd Even Linked List(Leetcode Problem 328)

Aim: To implement a program that rearranges a singly linked list so that all nodes at odd positions appear first, followed by all nodes at even positions, while preserving the relative order within each group.

Algorithm:

1.)Start with the head of the linked list.
2.)If the list has fewer than two nodes, return head.
3.)Initialize odd to the first node and even to the second node.
4.)Store the second node as evenHead to connect the even list later.
5.)While even and even->next exist:
   5.1)Connect the current odd node to the next odd node.
   5.2)Move odd to the next odd node.
   5.3)Connect the current even node to the next even node.
   5.4)Move even to the next even node.
6.)Connect the last odd node to evenHead.
7.)Return head.

Code:

class Solution {
public:
    ListNode* oddEvenList(ListNode* head) {
        if (!head || !head->next)
            return head;

        ListNode* odd = head;
        ListNode* even = head->next;
        ListNode* evenHead = even;

        while (even && even->next) {
            odd->next = even->next;
            odd = odd->next;

            even->next = odd->next;
            even = even->next;
        }

        odd->next = evenHead;

        return head;
    }
};
